#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093302} :activity/id #uuid "895b362d-6d8b-5264-b567-866f52b7c6d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050483 :topic/id #uuid "4d42e8ab-7d60-5380-b9d4-046ff9069092" :topic/math-academy-id 912} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093491 :tutorial/content "In many problems, we are interested in finding the **roots** of functions, that is, solving equations of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSometimes, this can be done **analytically**. For example, to find the roots of $f(x) = x^2 - 1$, we solve the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 - 1 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exact solutions $x = \\pm 1.$\n\nHowever, in many cases, finding the roots analytically is either too difficult or simply not possible. For example, the roots of the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2 + x \\sin{x} - 1\n$$\n\ncannot be found using standard algebraic techniques. When that happens, we turn to methods that provide good approximations rather than exact answers.\n\nIn the next slide, we will look at an example of how to apply such a method to this function." :tutorial/id #uuid "71728479-0258-589a-8972-828adf2b2ea0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7535 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "72821eb7-cd78-568d-9f90-3bbd6e3839d7" :step/math-academy-id 593950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093374 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll learn how **Newton’s method** (also known as the **Newton–Raphson method**) constructs such an approximation step by step. Our focus will be on the single-variable case, where the goal is to find a real number $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.\n\nTo apply Newton’s method, we begin by choosing an initial guess for the root, which we’ll denote by $x_0$. This guess might come from intuition or a rough sketch of the graph.\n\nBelow, we see the graph of $f(x)$ along with the initial guess $x_0$.\n\n![](images/e2/e2d794aa00d7b0d84a70344c89872aa48736182ff6dbf334d1201c0451c215b6.png)\n\nThe idea is to simplify the problem by replacing the curve with a straight line near our guess.\nRecall that the equation of the tangent line $y = F(x)$ to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $x = x_0$ is\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0).\n$$\n\nSince $F(x)$ closely approximates $f(x)$ near $x_0$, we solve the simpler linear equation $F(x) = 0$ instead of the original nonlinear equation $f(x) = 0$. This gives us a new estimate for the root that is easier to compute.\n\nTo find where this tangent intersects the $x$-axis, we set $F(x) = 0{:}$\n\n$$\n0 = f(x_0) + f'(x_0)(x - x_0)\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the term $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}({x}_{0})(x-{x}_{0}) & =-f({x}_{0}) \\\\ x-{x}_{0} & =-\\frac{f({x}_{0})}{{f}^{'}({x}_{0})} \\\\ x & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{f({x}_{0})}{{f}^{'}({x}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new value becomes our next guess, which we call $x_1$. So, we write\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_0 - \\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}.\n$$\n\nIn the figure below, the dashed line is the tangent at $x_0$. We follow it down to the $x$-axis to find our next guess, $x_1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e19765a4f180f1acc95c6460fb63b95ca9c4af811d13a3035d4e66ba26471a1.png)\n\nTo keep improving our estimate, we now repeat the same process, this time starting from $x_1$ instead of $x_0$. Applying the same idea gives the next guess\n\n$$\nx_2 = x_1 - \\frac{f(x_1)}{f'(x_1)}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e789ff357b039591d7bb00d05e48456f3e11ea172b9f04723a670101c1a1c15a.png)\n\nContinuing this process, we generate a sequence of values \n\n$$\nx_0, \\quad\nx_1, \\quad\nx_2, \\quad\nx_3, \n\\dots\n$$\n\n \nthat, under suitable conditions, converges to a root of $f(x)$.\nAt each step, the next approximation is computed using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nIn practice, we stop iterating when the change between consecutive guesses is very small, meaning we’re close enough to the actual root for our purposes." :tutorial/id #uuid "667de2d9-42aa-5db9-94b4-145c571f13e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13225 :tutorial/title "Newton's Method"} :step/id #uuid "b728d6c4-abd9-5e4a-9057-13f1a6631ed5" :step/math-academy-id 593951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093371 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0.1 x^2 - 3\n$$\n\nhas a root somewhere in the interval $[5, 6]$, and we want to use Newton’s method to estimate that root.\n\nWhile the root of $f(x)$ can be found analytically, we’ll apply Newton’s method to see how well it approximates the root after a few steps.\n\nThe formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (0.1 x^2-3)' = 0.2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{0.1{x}_{n}^{2}-3}{0.2{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(0.2{x}_{n})-(0.1{x}_{n}^{2}-3)}{0.2{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1{x}_{n}^{2}+3}{0.2{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}+30}{2{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe take $x_0 = 5$ as our initial guess, since it falls within the interval where the root is located:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}+30}{2{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{2}+30}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have the following updates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}+30}{2{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5.5)^{2}+30}{2(5.5)} \\\\  & =5.477\\,272… \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}+30}{2{x}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5.477\\,272…)^{2}+30}{2(5.477\\,272…)} \\\\  & =5.477\\,225…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx 5.477$ and $x_3 \\approx 5.477$ are the same when rounded to three decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we can take for the root of the function the value $x \\approx 5.477,$ rounded to three decimal places. The actual root is $x = \\sqrt{30} = 5.477\\,225 \\ldots,$ which is pretty close to our result just after $3$ iterations." :tutorial/id #uuid "3d1426e8-8d76-5c5c-8957-2ea9bc0606cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12764 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "28d8b753-9c24-5632-b4cf-26ab877d1b09" :step/math-academy-id 593952 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092370 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091937 :question/id #uuid "adb10509-7cc7-58fb-9c2c-5a219d0bec86" :question/math-academy-id "e-12154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050380}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292587}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"} :step/id #uuid "23e561de-79ba-553b-a21f-c5a2e949f9dc" :step/math-academy-id 593953 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092371 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091938 :question/id #uuid "35f589f7-0823-50bf-9bee-e2b9202bfda3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12155"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292586}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"} :step/id #uuid "1bba918e-ff00-58d4-894c-4ff7149c796f" :step/math-academy-id 593957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092372 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091939 :question/id #uuid "af6352ce-9fb6-5951-962a-8fe316f05c50" :question/math-academy-id "e-12156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314801}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"} :step/id #uuid "1b235a49-1012-5d60-95fb-188759fff70b" :step/math-academy-id 593960 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093373 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve seen how Newton’s method works in practice, it’s important to understand the conditions under which it actually succeeds. While the method can converge very quickly, it doesn’t always work as expected.\n\nFor Newton’s method to work reliably, a few key conditions need to be met:\n\n- The derivative $f'(x_n)$ must not be zero or close to zero.  \n\nIf the slope is flat, the tangent line becomes nearly horizontal, and the update step can either move very little or jump far in the wrong direction. In the figure below, we can see this effect in action. \n\nOur initial guess $x_1$ happens to lie in a region where the slope of the function is close to zero. If we follow the tangent line from that point to where it intersects the $x$-axis, the resulting guess $x_2$ ends up farther from the actual root than where we started ($d_1 < d_2$). This illustrates how a poor initial guess can lead Newton’s method in the wrong direction.\n\n![](images/8c/8cdfdcec6f1eab45635dd78e2b7ed567dca82dd4edc0fb65c0ad56e8678b6349.png)\n\n- The initial guess must be reasonably close to the root.  \n\nNewton’s method relies on local linear approximation. If the guess is too far from the root, the tangent might not reflect the behavior of the function in the right region.\n\n- The function must be smooth near the root.  \n\nSharp corners, discontinuities, or erratic changes in slope can break the assumptions behind the method. In such cases, the tangent might not be a good approximation even near the root.\n\nIf any of these conditions fail, Newton’s method can behave unpredictably. It may diverge entirely, bounce back and forth between values without settling down, converge to the wrong root, or get stuck in a region where the derivative is nearly zero and the update steps become ineffective." :tutorial/id #uuid "c3f1ef50-2c0c-5951-bcec-a03e3606ccc9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13124 :tutorial/title "Limitations of Newton’s Method"} :step/id #uuid "65b743ce-75a0-58a3-bafa-87a7d05ef58c" :step/math-academy-id 593963}] :activity/title "The Newton-Raphson Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092418}]}
